   280	
   281	- **Dispatch.** Out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch; A4.2 precedence holds inside.
   282	- **H1a's suite.** Forge, tamper, backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature, out-of-profile, the A9.1 mapping.
   283	  Degradation and wrapper instances move to their capabilities.
   284	- **Receipt anchor.** OTS stamps `SHA256(A_sig)`, tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`, which commits to both frames
   285	  and their exact detached signatures in manifest slot order (ENCODING-V1 §5, D-8, D-14). The verifier verifies the
   286	  committed signature bytes only. The frame-only layout is withdrawn, with no fallback.
   287	- **Issue order (D-15).** Fix manifest and metadata; build frames; sign; verify; **freeze** the signature bytes;
   288	  **stamp** `A_sig` (one stamp per receipt, R-3.42); obtain confirmation; ship. A signature made after T cannot fit
   289	  the anchor. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a fresh stamp. Signature identifiers stay independent
   290	  of signature bytes; the anchor digest may commit to them; nothing is normalized after stamping.
   291	- **Git stamp target (D-16).** The git channel's OTS stamp targets the **signed commit's object id**, never a tree,
   292	  blob or unsigned commit. The verifier recomputes the id, verifies `gpgsig`, follows commit→tree→blob, and checks
   293	  the stamped id. SHA-1 linkage remains a stated residual. The git evidence names its object format, and V1
   294	  accepts only `sha1`: the key-assertion repository stays in SHA-1 format at a host that supports it, whatever
   295	  git's default becomes (the author, 2026-10-13: Git 3 moves to SHA-256 in 2027; GitHub does not support it).
   296	- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose reliance
   297	  period contains that time validates it**. A KSK's reliance period runs from the start of its signing period to the
   298	  end of its signing period plus the maximum validity of the root DNSKEY RRSIGs it made, recorded with its source:
   299	  evidence it signed while signing stays usable for that long and no longer (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13, after
   300	  `docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F2). The rule is existential and order-independent: a candidate that fails is
   301	  informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible key has been tried (F1). A trusted KSK that
   302	  is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure. The trust configuration records signing and
   303	  reliance periods separately from publication and revocation dates.
   304	- **Trust configuration is append-only** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13). KSKs, header pins and repository keys are never
   305	  removed, and a closed period never changes. The repository key carries a signing period and is evaluated by the
   306	  same existential rule as the KSKs, so it can be replaced without failing old receipts or trusting a retired key
   307	  forever. **Rollover control:** a snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must pass
   308	  (SPIKE-REPORT, KSK-2024 re-capture; without it every receipt from 2026-10-11 would be falsely rejected).
   309	- **Custody.** V1 offers no renewal. Long-horizon validity depends on the dependency manifest and on the holder
   310	  keeping the exact bytes (a mirror that alters a signature byte breaks its copy, CODEX-REVIEW 5).
